  "worked_solution": "We begin with the graph of ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/16a1a985e5c0e15bf9d21cd70eaa63887bfa39dd81ef01720af2d1ea5d2b1516.png)\n\nNext, add $10$ to ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ to give ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10$, a vertical shift by $10$ units up. Point $(0,1)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ becomes $(0,11)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10.$ The horizontal asymptote becomes $y=10.$ The new graph is shown below.   \n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/af910cd3774ce1df9455df13bb1cd94354129b1127c921abb04013540889b41a.png)",
